<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for CHANNEL=store ONLY: the sandboxed build that goes to App
  Store Connect for TestFlight. The direct-download build (CHANNEL=direct,
  Developer ID + notarized) signs with NO entitlements file at all and must
  keep doing so — it is not sandboxed, deliberately, and pointing this file
  at it would containerise a shipping app behind the user's back.

  `$(TEAM_ID)` and `$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)` are substituted by
  packaging/macos/Makefile the same way Info.plist's are, from the values read
  out of the embedded provisioning profile rather than typed here. Signing an
  app whose `com.apple.application-identifier` disagrees with the profile is
  rejected on upload, so the profile is the single source and this file only
  names the shape.

  Four keys, and each one is here because something in the tree calls it. A
  capability nothing calls is not an entitlement we ask for — an unused
  entitlement is a question at review with no answer.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The two keys TestFlight is actually strict about, and the reason a
         Mac App Store app that never used TestFlight can ship without either.
         They must agree, exactly, with the embedded provisioning profile:
         App Store Connect refuses an upload whose application-identifier
         disagrees with the profile it was signed against. The Makefile
         substitutes both from the profile rather than from anything typed
         here, so there is one source and it is the profile. -->
    <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
    <string>$(TEAM_ID).$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
    <string>$(TEAM_ID)</string>

    <!-- The switch itself. Required at upload: App Store Connect's asset
         validation rejects a macOS build whose executables lack it, with
         "App sandbox not enabled ... must include the
         'com.apple.security.app-sandbox' entitlement with a Boolean value of
         true" (ITMS-90296). That gate sits in front of TestFlight, not after
         it, so there is no non-sandboxed route to a beta build. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Outbound sockets. Everything the app draws is fetched: the NEXRAD
         archive and chunk streams, basemap and terrain tiles, HRRR, MRMS,
         GMGSI, GLM, SPC, METAR and the NWS alert/zone feeds, plus the
         `ewebsock` chunk notifier (squallar-radar/src/chunk_notify.rs:267,
         wss://nexrad-aws-notifier.mcswain.dev). Without it every connect
         fails and the map is empty. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- The serial GPS reader. This variant is built from the same binaries as
         the direct download, so `serialport` is compiled in and the port
         picker is drawn; without this entitlement `available_ports()` returns
         Err and the dropdown renders empty forever
         (squallar-nmea-serial/src/serial.rs:30).

         Asked for rather than designed around. It is a documented, unrestricted
         App Sandbox entitlement ("Interaction with serial devices" in Apple's
         Entitlement Key Reference), not one of the temporary exceptions that
         invite a review question. The alternative was a second pair of
         cross-compiles with the reader compiled out, and the feature that
         would have taken could not be spelled without also turning serialport
         on for iOS, where it is a link error no test catches. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.serial</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- CoreLocation. squallar-location/src/os_location/apple.rs calls
         CLLocationManager::new() (:378), requestWhenInUseAuthorization()
         (:419) and startUpdatingLocation() (:199) for real; the app uses the
         fix to place the user and pick the nearest radar site.

         This one fails SILENTLY without the entitlement, which is why it is
         worth naming: requestWhenInUseAuthorization returns `void`, so a
         denied request produces no dialog, no error at the call site and no
         fix — the same shape the Info.plist comment already documents for a
         missing usage-description key. NSLocationUsageDescription is present
         (Info.plist) and is the other half; neither works alone. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Not here, and each for a reason:

         * `com.apple.security.network.server` — every TcpListener::bind in
           the tree is a loopback stub inside a #[cfg(test)] module. Nothing
           listens in a shipping build.
         * `com.apple.security.files.user-selected.*`, `files.bookmarks.*`,
           `files.downloads.*` — there is no file dialog, no drag-and-drop
           and no path the user names. Every persistence root is derived from
           $HOME (squallar/src/platform.rs:85-136: config, zone cache,
           basemap block cache, basemap downloads), and the sandbox redirects
           $HOME into ~/Library/Containers/<bundle-id>/Data, so those four
           roots land inside the container with no entitlement at all.
         * `com.apple.security.device.usb`, `.bluetooth`, `.camera`,
           `.audio-input`, `personal-information.addressbook`/`.calendars`,
           `print`, `scripting-targets` — no code, no dependency.
         * Anything for GPU/Metal — there is no such entitlement. wgpu talks
           to Metal in-process and the sandbox does not gate it. The capacity
           reader's mach_host_self()/host_statistics64
           (squallar/src/capacity/apple.rs:102) is the task's own host port,
           not a bootstrap lookup of a named service, so it needs no
           mach-lookup exception either.
         * `com.apple.security.cs.*` (allow-jit, disable-library-validation,
           allow-unsigned-executable-memory) — these are hardened-runtime
           exceptions, not sandbox keys. Nothing in this tree JITs or loads an
           unsigned library; the direct-download build already runs under the
           hardened runtime with none of them. -->
</dict>
</plist>
